- **Already Cited in P4:** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Measured trait variation across wild soil bacteria using quantitative stable isotope probing (qSIP). Proved that **phylogeny explains 31% to 58% of physiological trait variance within ecosystems**, and up to **38% across global biomes**.
- **Relevance to P4:** Provides the field benchmark for P4's variance partitioning: host lineage explaining $72\%$ of dark-family repertoire variance is consistent with the deep phylogenetic conservatism governing wild soil bacteria.

### 12. Knight et al. (2024) — Phylogenetic Signal in Extreme Stress Responses (Nature)
- **Title:** Soil microbiomes show consistent and predictable responses to extreme events
- **Authors:** Christopher G. Knight, et al., Franca C. J. Bongers, Richard D. Bardgett
- **Year / Journal:** 2024 | *Nature*, 636:690–696
- **DOI:** [`10.1038/s41586-024-08185-3`](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08185-3)
- **Publisher URL:** [https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08185-3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08185-3)
- **Full Text:** [`12_Knight_2024_SoilMicrobiomesExtremeEvents.pdf`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/05_ecology_design/12_Knight_2024_SoilMicrobiomesExtremeEvents.pdf) & [`.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/05_ecology_design/12_Knight_2024_SoilMicrobiomesExtremeEvents.xml)
- **Already Cited in P4:** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Evaluated 30 European field sites subjected to severe climate shocks (drought, heat, flood, freeze). Demonstrated that resistance and resilience to abiotic stress are **deeply phylogenetically conserved** (Pagel's $\lambda$, $P < 0.001$), triggering whole-lineage community sorting rather than idiosyncratic drift.
- **Relevance to P4:** Explains why P4 observes $97.9\%$ lineage fidelity in dark families and why environmental covariates explain only $11\%$. Extreme abiotic stress acts as a macro-evolutionary filter on whole lineages (species turnover) rather than selecting fine-scale physiological plastic adjustments.

